= Justification — Simple =

In epistemology, '''justification''' means having an appropriately good basis for a [[Belief/Simple|belief]].

Suppose two people both believe the same true fact.

One person has strong [[Evidence/Simple|evidence]].

The other guessed.

Their beliefs have the same truth value, but they do not have the same epistemic support.

Philosophers disagree about what makes a belief justified.

Some focus on reasons or evidence that the person can access.

Others focus on whether the process that produced the belief is reliable.

Another important difference is between:

* having good reasons available;
* actually believing something because of those reasons.

A belief can also lose justification when new information shows that the evidence was misleading or the method was unreliable. This new information is called a '''defeater'''.

Justification is related to [[Knowledge/Simple|knowledge]], but a justified belief can still be false.

